Make both layers agree on retry, and give the signing path real backoff (#87, #93) (#152)

* fix(mcp): agree with the ledger on connection-lifecycle SQLSTATEs (#87)

classifyStorage treated every non-constraint SQLSTATE as not retryable,
including 57P01/57P02/57P03 (operator intervention) and 08006/08003
(connection exception) - the exact codes a stale pooled connection gets
when Postgres is genuinely killed. internal/ledger.classify already
recognised those classes as a dependency outage and reported retryable
true, so the same real outage produced opposite retry advice depending
on which layer's pool answered first (ADR-0016 SS2, SS5).

Move classifyStorage into errors.go alongside the rest of the
classification logic, narrow it only on evidence (the same SQLSTATE
class prefixes internal/ledger.classify already treats as retryable),
and add *ledger.StoreError as an explicit case in Classify so a ledger
failure that reaches it unwrapped carries its own classification
across instead of defaulting to INVARIANT_VIOLATION. *ledger.StoreError
cannot literally implement mcp.Classified - ErrorClass's signature
names mcp.Class, and internal/ledger importing internal/mcp would
cycle - so this mirrors the existing *spire.Error special case instead.

* test(mcp,ledger): pin the connection-lifecycle SQLSTATE agreement (#87)

MCP-020: classifyStorage now reports LEDGER_UNAVAILABLE retryable=true
for 57P01, 57P02, 57P03, 08006 and 08003 - the codes RM-028 (#36) found
it disagreeing with internal/ledger.classify on. Confirmed against the
pre-fix code first: all five failed with "retryable is false, want
true" for the expected reason before the classifyStorage fix landed.

LED-014: extends internal/ledger's own table with the two
connection-lifecycle codes (57P02, 57P03, 08003) it wasn't already
exercising, alongside the two it already covered (57P01, 08006).
classify already got these right; this just proves it and keeps the
set exercised on both sides equal.

* fix(signing): back off Rekor lookup retries with growing, jittered intervals (#93)

RM-070: IP §6.3 asks for bounded retries with backoff and jitter; the
Rekor lookup in findRekorEntry slept a flat rekorLookupDelay (RM-034
measured four gaps identical to within 1ms). Turn rekorLookupDelay
into the Base of an exponential backoff via internal/segment's
RetryPolicy shape (reused, not reinvented — same shape RM-012 built
for Rekor anchoring), and add equal jitter on top via a new
substitutable rekorWait. Multiplier is pinned at 2 so gap n is always
strictly less than gap n+1 by construction, whatever the random draw.

Adds SIG-009..SIG-012 (uncatalogued unit tests, not added to doc 07):
growth is structural, two injected random sequences produce different
interval sequences (RM-034's withheld jitter assertion, driven rather
than waited for), the retry loop itself with the wait faked out
(attempts and error class unchanged), and cancellation during a
backoff stops the loop immediately.

