* test(contract): stop tolerating the transitional classification (#87)

settleOutage used to only log a "transitional" SQLSTATE-carrying answer
from either layer on the way to a settled outage, because RM-028 (#36)
found the two disagreeing on it. RM-067 (#87) fixed classifyStorage, so
every answer - settled or not - is now asserted to agree with
internal/ledger via mcp.Classify, and the function fails loudly if one
ever does not. Verified live against a real killed Postgres: both
layers now report SQLSTATE 57P01 as LEDGER_UNAVAILABLE retryable=true
on the very first, transitional call.

* fix(signing): silence gosec G404 on the jitter's random source (#93)

math/rand/v2 is flagged as a weak PRNG by gosec, correctly for
security-sensitive uses; the Rekor lookup's backoff jitter isn't one —
predictability here only affects retry timing, not anything an
attacker could exploit.

* test(mcp): give MCP-021 more rounds against heavy Docker contention (#87)

A full -race run of this package alongside a second agent's own
container-heavy tests (SPIRE/Sigstore stacks, per #100/#101's known
address-pool pressure) once burned all 8 rounds without a single round
landing inside the graceful-shutdown window on both layers - the same
window this repo's own settleOutage narrows for. In isolation, or on a
quieter run, 1-4 rounds is typical. Raised the bound to 24: cheap on a
quiet machine (the loop returns on the first successful round), and
buys back the margin contention costs on a busy one.

* fix(signing): assert growth and jitter in the SIG-004 Rekor wire test (#93)

RM-034 (#42) deliberately withheld two assertions from SIG-004's
Rekor-slow case: intervals grow, and repeated attempts do not produce
identical intervals — a jitter assertion would have been red against
code it had no mandate to change. #93 is that mandate; add both,
against internal/signing's new backoff+jitter (previous commit).

Per-attempt floors replace the old flat wantDelay (rekorWait's equal
jitter guarantees backoff/2 as a genuine floor); growth is asserted
first-vs-last with a 3x margin rather than every adjacent pair, since
this measures real wire time through a proxy and the requested-wait
growth already has deterministic, network-noise-free coverage in
internal/signing (TestRekorWaitJitteredGapsAlwaysGrow); and the
original defect — all four gaps within 1ms of each other — is refuted
directly.

